{"subject":"Microbiology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::cereb::subject_tests::cereb sub test Microbiology::009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204732","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":1,"stem":"Which of the following is/are dimorphic fungi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"},{"label":"C","text":"Aspergillus species"},{"label":"D","text":"Rhizopus species"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Histoplasma capsulatum","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" lang=\"PT-BR\" style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. B. Histoplasma capsulatum</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204734","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":2,"stem":"Boggy swelling of scalp in children is caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichophyton mentagrophyte"},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophyton verrucosum"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsporum canis"},{"label":"D","text":"Malassezia furfur"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Microsporum canis","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Ans. C. Microsporum canis</strong></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">The boggy swelling of the scalp in children is typically associated with tinea capitis (a fungal infection of the scalp</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">Microsporum canis is a common causative agent of tinea capitis, particularly in children. It often causes boggy swelling of the scalp, which can be associated with kerion (a severe inflammatory reaction).</span><br/> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">It is typically transmitted by contact with infected animals (especially cats and dogs).</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">Though other dermatophytes can also cause boggy swelling of the scalp but microspore canis is the most common cause in children.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Other options:</strong></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option A. Trichophyton mentagrophyte</strong>: = can cause tinea corporis (ringworm of the body) and other dermatophyte infections, it is less common in causing tinea capitis in children.</span><br/> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option B. Trichophyton verrucosum</strong>: commonly associated with ringworm of the body or feet and is typically transmitted through contact with infected cattle</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option D</strong>. malassezia furfur: causative agent of tinea versicolor, which causes hypopigmented or hyperpigmented patches on the skin, primarily on the trunk. It does not cause boggy scalp swelling.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Educational Objective</strong>: </span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">Microsporum canis is the most common cause of boggy scalp swelling (kerion) in children with tinea capitis, typically acquired from infected pets.</span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204736","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":3,"stem":"Which of the following organisms is mplicated in the causation of botryomycosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus albus"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcus pneumonia"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Staphylococcus aureus","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. A. Staphylococcus aureus</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204738","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":4,"stem":"Sclerotic bodies are found in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinosporidiosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Coccidiomycosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Chromoblastomycosis"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Chromoblastomycosis","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. D. Chromoblastomycosis</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204740","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":5,"stem":"Rhinosporidium seeberi belongs to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fungus"},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteria"},{"label":"C","text":"Aquatic protistan protozoa"},{"label":"D","text":"Virus"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Aquatic protistan protozoa","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. C. Aquatic protistan protozoa</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204742","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":6,"stemHtml":"Histopathological image given below belongs to which of the following fungus?<br/><img src=\"https://cerebellum-web-static.s3.amazonaws.com/media/public/images/2023/08/18/untitled-13506.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blastomyces"},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasma"},{"label":"C","text":"Coccidioides"},{"label":"D","text":"Paracoccidioides"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Blastomyces","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans.</span></strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"> <strong>A. Blastomyces </strong></span></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204744","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":7,"stemHtml":"The image given below belongs which of the following organism?<br/><img src=\"https://cerebellum-web-static.s3.amazonaws.com/media/public/images/2023/08/18/untitled-13508.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptococcus"},{"label":"B","text":"Candida"},{"label":"C","text":"Histoplasma"},{"label":"D","text":"Blastomyces"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Cryptococcus","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. A. Cryptococcus</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204746","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":8,"stem":"Which of the organisms is responsible for orbital cellulitis in a patient with diabetic ketoacidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptococcus"},{"label":"B","text":"Candida"},{"label":"C","text":"Mucor"},{"label":"D","text":"Aspergillus"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Mucor","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. C. Mucor</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204748","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":9,"stem":"In a patient, corneal scraping reveals narrow angled septate hyphae, which of the following is likely etiologic agent:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucor"},{"label":"B","text":"Aspergillus"},{"label":"C","text":"Histoplasma"},{"label":"D","text":"Candida"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Aspergillus","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. B. Aspergillus</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204750","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":10,"stem":"Spaghetti and meatballs appearance colonies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida"},{"label":"B","text":"Dermatophytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptococcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Malassezia furfur"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Malassezia furfur","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. D. Malassezia furfur</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204752","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":11,"stem":"A gardener has multiple vesicles on hand and multiple eruptions along the lymphatics. Fungus responsible is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporothrix schenckii"},{"label":"B","text":"Cladosporium"},{"label":"C","text":"Histoplama"},{"label":"D","text":"Candida"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Sporothrix schenckii","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. A. Sporothrix schenckii</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204754","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":12,"stem":"An HIV patient present with cough for 10 days. Sputum culture by SDA reveals broad budding yeast with double contoured cell wall. Diagnosis:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histoplasmosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Coccidioidomycosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Blastomycosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillium marneffei"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Blastomycosis","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. C. Blastomycosis</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204756","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":13,"stemHtml":"A patient presented with complaints of dysphagia.  On UGI Endoscopy a diagnosis of esophageal candidiasis was made. The organism obtained from the sample was cultured on a specialized media and the below finding were visible on microscopy of the culture.  What phenomenon is shown below?<br/><img src=\"images/image_671_66_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germ tube formation"},{"label":"B","text":"Spore formation"},{"label":"C","text":"Mould to yeast formation"},{"label":"D","text":"Budding"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Germ tube formation","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. A. Germ tube formation</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204758","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":14,"stem":"Which of the following is aseptate fungus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspergillus"},{"label":"B","text":"Candida"},{"label":"C","text":"Nocardia"},{"label":"D","text":"Rhizopus"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Rhizopus","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. D. Rhizopus</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204760","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":15,"stem":"All are yeast or yeast like fungi except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida"},{"label":"B","text":"Saccharomyces"},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptococcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyton"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Trichophyton","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. D. Trichophyton</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204762","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":16,"stem":"All statements are true about mycetoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eumycetoma is caused by bacteria"},{"label":"B","text":"Surgery is important component of treatment"},{"label":"C","text":"Usually painless"},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnosis can be made by examination of lesion"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Eumycetoma is caused by bacteria","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. A. Eumycetoma is caused by bacteria</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204764","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":17,"stemHtml":"A Female had a thorn prick 5 years ago.  She presented with a slowly growing 2 × 2 cm verrucous lesion which on KOH mount showed the following image?<br/><img src=\"images/image_669_63_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phaeohyphomycosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Blastomycosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Sporotrichosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Chromoblastomycosis"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"Chromoblastomycosis","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. D. Chromoblastomycosis</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204766","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":18,"stemHtml":"HIV patient presented with fever and diarrhea for 3 weeks. Patient was started on Sulphamethoxazole-trimethoprim.  His diarrhea responded but he continued to have fever.  Bone marrow aspirate showed the following picture.  What is not correct for the fungal organism?<br/><img src=\"images/image_669_61_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an intracellular yeast"},{"label":"B","text":"It does not grow on SDA medium"},{"label":"C","text":"It can cause disseminated infection"},{"label":"D","text":"Infective agent is spores"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"It does not grow on SDA medium","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. B. It does not grow on SDA medium</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204768","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":19,"stem":"A vitreous aspirate from a case of metastatic endophthalmitis on culture yields Gram-positive round to oval cells, 12-14 µm in size. The aspirate on Gram staining shows the presence of pseudohyphae. Which of the following is the most likely etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspergillus"},{"label":"B","text":"Rhizopus"},{"label":"C","text":"Candida"},{"label":"D","text":"Fusarium"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Candida","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><strong><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Ans. C. Candida</span></strong></span></span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436204770","topic":"Cerebellum · 009-T-20 Microbiology Test-7 - 18-08-2023 - 2023-08-18","number":20,"stemHtml":"Identify the fungal organism in this slide stained with Gomori methenamine silver stain:<br/><img src=\"images/image_669_60_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute angle branching, Rhizopus"},{"label":"B","text":"Right angle branching, Aspergillus"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute angle branching, Aspergillus"},{"label":"D","text":"Right angle branching, Mucor"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Acute angle branching, Aspergillus","solutionHtml":"<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Ans. C. Acute angle branching, Aspergillus</strong></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain — a common stain used in histopathology to identify fungal organisms — we can differentiate fungi based on hyphal structure, branching angle, and presence or absence of septa.</span><br/><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">The image shows thin, septate hypae with acute angle branching (dichotomous branching at acute angles) suggestive of an infection with Aspergillus species.  </span><br/> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Other options:</strong></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option A. Acute angle branching</strong>, <strong>Rhizopus</strong>: this is false as Rhizopus shows right angle branching and aseptate hyphae, not acute.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option B. Right angle branching</strong>, <strong>Aspergillus</strong>: this is false, Aspergillus shows acute angle branching, not right angle.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Option D. Right angle branching</strong>, <strong>Mucor</strong>: this is false as Mucor shows right angle branching and aseptate hyphae, not acute.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\"><strong>Educational Objective:</strong></span></p><p> </p><p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;\">Aspergillus shows septate hyphae with acute-angle branching on GMS stain, while Mucorales (Rhizopus/Mucor) display aseptate hyphae with right-angle branching.</span></p><br/><br/>","tags":""}]}